<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Sao</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Encountering Sorrow</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Li Sao (Encountering Sorrow) is a long poem of about 370 lines attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qu-yuan">Qu Yuan</a>, minister of the State of Chu in the late fourth century BCE, and the opening work of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chu-ci">Chu Ci</a> anthology. In it a loyal noble slandered at court laments his fate and makes a visionary journey through the heavens in search of a worthy ruler. It is the longest poem of pre-imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and a model for later Chinese poetry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/li-sao.jpg" alt="Li Sao" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: White whirlwind · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Li_sao_illustr%C3%A9_(crop).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The poem is traditionally attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qu-yuan">Qu Yuan</a> of the Kingdom of Chu, who died about 278 BCE, and is placed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-huai-of-chu">King Huai of Chu</a>. Its precise date is unknown: it seems to have been written after Qu Yuan's exile by King Huai, but before the king's captivity in the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> began in 299 BCE. Unlike the anonymous voices of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry">Shijing</a>, its speaker appears as a poetic character, and the rest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chu-ci">Chuci</a> anthology is centred on it as the purported biography of its author.</p><p>The meaning of the title is disputed. The biography of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qu-yuan">Qu Yuan</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> explains li sao as li you, leaving with sorrow, while the 1st-century CE scholar Ban Gu glossed it as encountering sorrow. Modern theories suggest that scribes mistook one character for another, so that the name may once have meant to encounter a fault.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The poem opens with the poet introducing himself, his descent from the god-king Gao-yang, the names his father gave him and his former glory. He laments that factions at court have slandered him and that his lord has rejected him, then recounts a series of shamanistic spirit journeys through real and mythological landscapes, teaming jade-white dragons, ordering the sun-driver Xi-he to slow her pace and watering his horses at the Pools of Xian. Its imagery draws on the culture of Chu, strongly associated with a Chinese form of shamanism.</p><p>At the end the poet resolves to leave and join Peng Xian. The Han commentator Wang Yi took Peng Xian to be a Shang dynasty official who drowned himself after the king rejected his advice; others read the name as two people, or as an ancient shaman.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>With 373 lines and close to 2500 characters, the poem is written in the fu style and is a seminal work in the Chinese tradition of landscape and travel literature. Later criticism and allegorical reading sometimes exaggerated its theme of the righteous minister unjustly rejected. In the 19th century the linguist Pan Zuyin reissued it in four volumes. It was translated into English by E. H. Parker in 1878-1879 and later by James Legge, David Hawkes and Stephen Owen, and into French by the Marquis d'Hervey de Saint Denys in 1870.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Li Sao” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Songs of Chu (Chuci), compiled in the Han dynasty and headed by the <b>Li Sao</b> attributed to Qu Yuan, minister of King Huai, is with the Book of Songs one of the two fountainheads of Chinese poetry, and the legend of Qu Yuan's suicide in the Miluo…</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
